+// Render interpolation.
 //
 // The simulation runs in 30 Hz ticks; with the frame rate unlocked several
-// frames are drawn per tick. After every tick we snapshot each object's world
-// space node matrices (what the renderer draws skinned and rigid models from)
-// and position. Once a frame's ticks are done we write a blend of the last two
-// snapshots into the objects, weighted by the fraction of a tick that has
-// elapsed since the last one (game_time_globals + 0x14). The per-frame camera
-// and the renderer then see smooth motion. Before the next tick runs, the
-// real state is written back so the simulation never sees blended values.
+// frames are drawn per tick. After every tick we snapshot what the per-frame
+// camera and the renderer read from the simulation:
+// - every object's position, facing (forward and up) and world space node
+//   matrices;
+// - every local player's first-person weapon and arms pose: camera space node
+//   matrices and the animation's camera node, which live outside the objects.
+// Once a frame's ticks are done we write a blend of the last two snapshots,
+// weighted by the fraction of a tick that has elapsed since the last one
+// (game_time_globals + 0x14). The camera, the first-person view and the
+// renderer then see smooth motion. Before the next tick runs, the real state
+// is written back so the simulation never sees blended values. If something
+// changed a value in between (a respawn, say), its value is kept instead.
+//
+// The ticks also replace the cameras' copy of each player's facing with the
+// facing quantized for the simulation (13-bit yaw, 12-bit pitch), while frames
+// without a tick use the exact facing. The exact facing is put back after the
+// ticks so the view doesn't shimmer at 30 Hz.
 
